      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/api.php?action=query&prop=extracts&explaintext=1&titles=2026+United+States+House+of+Representatives+elections+in+California&format=json&redirects=1",
      "fetched_at": "2026-11-05T11:18:28.555743Z",
      "sha256": "0dcf8b306c987f12b606dd0bede68985aa5038bbcc3b7f2828fa7d293d227d13",
      "role": "report",
      "note": "wikipedia extract title='2026 United States House of Representatives elections in California'",
      "text": "The 2026 United States House of Representatives elections in California were held on November 3, 2026, to elect the 52 U.S. representatives from the State of California, one from each of the state's congressional districts. The elections coincided with other elections to the House of Representatives, elections to the United States Senate, and various state and local elections. The passage of Proposition 50 in 2025 significantly altered many districts. The primary elections took place on June 2, 2026.\n\n\n== Redistricting ==\n\n\n=== Background ===\nIn November 2025, votes decisively approved a constitutional amendment to allow for mid-decade redistricting and to instate a map that would have likely given Democrats 5 additional seats in the House of Representatives. The map defined in Proposition 50 is a Democratic gerrymander intended to offset the gerrymander by Texas Republicans, both of which are part of the broader 2025–2026 United States redistricting effort. It redraws several congressional districts to incorporate larger shares of urban and suburban Democratic voters, increasing Democratic registration advantages in competitive districts and converting several Republican-leaning seats into Democratic-leaning ones. Republicans have responded to Proposition 50 with legislation, their own propositions, and litigation. Republicans challenged the proposition in court both before the election, as well as after it was passed by the voters, but their legal efforts were exhausted when the Supreme Court of the United States refused to hear the appeal.\n\n\n== Overview ==\n\n\n== Statewide polling ==\n\n\n=== Statewide congressional polls ===\n\n\n== Districts 1–26 ==\n\n\n== Districts 27–52 ==\n\n\n== Notes ==\n\n\n== References ==\n\n\n== External links ==\nCalifornia Secretary of State\n\nPrimary Election - June 2, 2026\nGeneral Election - November 3, 2026"
    },
    {
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/api.php?action=query&prop=extracts&explaintext=1&titles=California%27s+34th+congressional+district&format=json&redirects=1",
      "fetched_at": "2026-11-05T11:18:28.888852Z",
      "sha256": "412b8f52a8ca0022a8ef4317ba7ed6ef5a56165b3a9fcbfb7678f31072f7c904",
